# Algebraic Data Types { .heading-no-number }

> For a progressive introduction, see [The Koka Book: Your Own Types](../tutorial/types.html).
> This guide is a quick-reference for data modeling patterns.

Koka supports rich user-defined types: records (`struct`), sum types with multiple
constructors (`type`), and combinations of the two. Pattern matching lets you
destructure any of these cleanly. This guide covers the full range of type definitions
available in Koka.

---

## Structs

A `struct` defines a product type with named fields. Koka auto-generates a constructor function and field accessors:

```koka
struct person
  age : int
  name : string
  realname : string = name

val brian = Person( 29, "Brian" )
```

Fields can have default values (`realname = name`). The constructor accepts both positional and named arguments:

```koka
val alice = Person( name = "Alice", age = 30 )
```

Field accessors use dot notation (`brian.age` is just `age(brian)`):

```koka
fun example-fields()
  println(brian.name ++ " is " ++ brian.age.show ++ " years old")
```

### Updating Structs

All structs (and all Koka data) are _immutable_. To produce a modified version, use the
_application syntax_ on a value — passing named fields to override:

```koka

val brian = Person( 29, "Brian" )
////
fun birthday( p : person ) : person
  p( age = p.age + 1 )

fun example-birthday()
  println( brian.birthday.age )
```

The expression `p(age = p.age + 1)` creates a new `:person` value equal to `p` but
with `age` incremented. This is the standard, idiomatic way to produce updated
values in Koka — no mutation, no special update syntax, just function application.

---


## Sum Types (Alternatives)

A `type` declaration with multiple constructors is a _sum type_ (a.k.a. union or variant):

```koka
type color
  Red
  Green
  Blue
```

Constructors can carry data:

```koka
type number
  Infinity
  Integer( i : int )
```

Create values as `Integer(42)` or `Infinity`.

---

## Polymorphic and Recursive Types

Types can be parameterized and recursive. The standard list type is:

```unchecked
type list<a>
  Nil
  Cons{ head : a; tail : list<a> }
```

Access named fields with dot notation: `Cons(1,Nil).head` evaluates to `1`.

---

## Pattern Matching

Use `match` to deconstruct ADT values:

```koka
fun describe(c : color) : string
  match c
    Red   -> "red"
    Green -> "green"
    Blue  -> "blue"
```

Patterns can bind variables and nest arbitrarily:

```koka
fun head-of-list(xs : list<int>) : string
  match xs
    Nil       -> "empty"
    Cons(x,_) -> "first element: " ++ x.show
```

Koka warns on non-exhaustive patterns at compile time.

---

## The `struct` / `type` Relationship

`struct` is syntactic sugar for a `type` with a single constructor:

```unchecked
// These are equivalent:
struct person { age : int; name : string }

type person
  Person { age : int; name : string }
```

---

## Special Built-in Types

| Type | Constructors | Notes |
|------|-------------|-------|
| `bool` | `True`, `False` | Boolean |
| `()` | `()` | Unit (one value) |
| `void` | _(none)_ | Uninhabited type |
| `list<a>` | `Nil`, `Cons` | Singly-linked list |
| `maybe<a>` | `Nothing`, `Just(a)` | Optional value |

---

## Value Types

Small, non-recursive structs are automatically stack-allocated as value types.
You can force this with the `value` keyword:

```koka
value struct argb{ alpha: int; color-red: int; color-green: int; color-blue: int }
```

Value types avoid heap allocation and reference counting overhead for small data.

